::ALG2_U02_L01_GP01::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L01_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.3
Question:
What is the degree of f(x) = 3x² - 4x + 9?
{
=A. 2 #Correct. The highest exponent is 2.
~B. 3 #This is the leading coefficient.
~C. 4 #There is no fourth power.
~D. 9 #This is the constant term.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_GP02::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L01_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.3
Question:
Which form best shows the zeros of f(x) = (x - 5)(x + 2)?
{
~A. standard form #Standard form does not show factors directly.
~B. vertex form #Vertex form shows the vertex.
=C. factored form #Correct. Factored form shows zeros.
~D. table form #A table may suggest zeros but not structure.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_GP03::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L01_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.3
Question:
What are the zeros of f(x) = x² - 7x + 12?
{
~A. x \= -3 and x \= -4 #The signs are reversed.
=B. x \= 3 and x \= 4 #Correct. It factors as (x - 3)(x - 4).
~C. x \= -7 and x \= 12 #These are coefficients.
~D. x \= 0 and x \= 12 #The constant is not automatically a zero.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_GP04::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L01_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.3
Question:
What is the y-intercept of f(x) = 2x² - 6x + 5?
{
~A. 2 #This is the leading coefficient.
~B. -6 #This is the linear coefficient.
~C. 0 #f(0) is not 0.
=D. 5 #Correct. f(0) = 5.
}

::ALG2_U02_L01_GP05::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L01_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.PF.3
Question:
What is the vertex of g(x) = (x - 3)² + 8?
{
~A. (-3, 8) #The sign is reversed.
=B. (3, 8) #Correct. Vertex is (3, 8).
~C. (8, 3) #Coordinates are reversed.
~D. (3, -8) #The k-value is positive.
}

